<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Using Bivariate Data in Context</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00775</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Using Bivariate Data in Context</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2f9cf4dd-9506-48d1-9b30-d5b696ec4468</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up</title>
      <para id="para-00001">In the following activity, you will complete a series of questions regarding scatter
        plots and the variables that represent the data. </para>
      <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00002">Log into <link url="https://student.desmos.com">student.desmos.com</link> using the
        information provided by your teacher to complete the activity.</para>
      </note>

      <para id="para-00003">Examine the unlabeled graphs below, then match the variables to the scatter plot you think they best fit. Choose from among the following variable combinations. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.
        <table class="unnumbered" id="table-00001">
          <tgroup cols="1">
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = average number of free throws shot in a season<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = basketball team score per game
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = daily low temperature in Celsius for Denver, CO<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = boxes of cereal in stock at a grocery in Miami, FL
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = measured student height in feet<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = measured student height in inches
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = average number of minutes spent in a waiting room<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = hospital satisfaction rating
                </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
      </para>

      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">1. Which variables best describe the relationship represented in the graph below?</para>
          <media alt=".">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_05_01_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005"><m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = daily low temperature in Celsius for Denver, CO<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = boxes of cereal in stock at a grocery in Miami, FL</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">2. Which variables best describe the relationship represented in the graph below?</para>
          <media alt=".">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_05_01_02.svg"/>
          </media>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007"><m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = average number of free throws shot in a season<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = basketball team score per game</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">3. Which variables best describe the relationship represented in the graph below?</para>
          <media alt=".">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_05_01_03.svg"/>
          </media>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">
            <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = measured student height in feet<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = measured student height in inches
          </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00010">4. Which variables best describe the relationship represented in the graph below?</para>
          <media alt=".">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_05_01_04.svg"/>
          </media>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00011"><m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = average number of minutes spent in a waiting room<newline/>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:math> variable = hospital satisfaction rating</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>